   (a) AS DECLARED, the counter family [1, 1] (the birth order the residue order):
       a = 0: the + fraction among B's first N/4 births 0.297; runs in the whole list 4
       a = N/4: the + fraction among B's first N/4 births 1.000; runs in the whole list 4
   (b) UNDER A SEED-SET ORDER (the mixing hash), 200 seeds, the decoders fixed before the run, each right on: first quarter 196 of 400 (0.490); threshold 200 of 400 (0.500); runs (few = the declared order) 200 of 400 (0.500); lag-one sameness 214 of 400 (0.535); halves' drift 191 of 400 (0.477); the runs about 128 (the balanced string's own)
N = 2048: the counts of + at b = 3N/8 1024 and 1024 of 2048 (exact);
   with the residues known the two lists differ on 1448 of 2048 births = 0.70703 (E_N; Inside, Bell's theorem)
   (a) AS DECLARED, the counter family [1, 1] (the birth order the residue order):
       a = 0: the + fraction among B's first N/4 births 0.293; runs in the whole list 4
       a = N/4: the + fraction among B's first N/4 births 1.000; runs in the whole list 4
   (b) UNDER A SEED-SET ORDER (the mixing hash), 200 seeds, the decoders fixed before the run, each right on: first quarter 208 of 400 (0.520); threshold 200 of 400 (0.500); runs (few = the declared order) 200 of 400 (0.500); lag-one sameness 188 of 400 (0.470); halves' drift 210 of 400 (0.525); the runs about 1024 (the balanced string's own)
(c) THE THEOREM: for a uniformly random permutation of the births, B's list at fixed b is a balanced multiset (N/2 pluses for either a) in uniformly random order, so its distribution is the uniform distribution over balanced strings for a and for a' alike; no decoder fixed before the run, without the seed, does better than 1/2: exact for a uniformly random order; the seed-set shuffle is its integer form, closed to a reader without the seed. The Inside dependence (E_N of the residues) is Bell's theorem and stays.
